data: {"type": "action", "id": "act3", "icon": "check_documentation", "title": "Fetched workflow", "results": [{"icon": "", "title": "Workflow"}], "results_units": "Workflows", "ended_at": "2026-08-01T09:00:06.702146Z", "output": "## **The Main Prompt**\n\n```plain text\n\nPull two datasets.\n\nFirst, from Google Search Console, give me the top 50 queries we're\n\nranking for — the actual words people searched before clicking through\n\nto our site.\n\nSecond, from Google Ads, give me the top 50 highest-converting keywords\n\nfrom the last 90 days. Note that the Google Ads API returns keyword-level\n\ndata (the bidded keywords), not raw search terms — use this as a proxy\n\nfor buyer intent and flag the match type for each keyword so we can\n\nweight exact and phrase matches more heavily.\n\nNow compare both lists against the actual copy on our homepage. Identify:\n\n1. Which high-volume or high-converting keywords contain words or phrases\n\n   that appear nowhere in our homepage copy\n\n2. Which words appear prominently on our homepage but never show up in\n\n   search queries\n\n3. Which buyer-intent keywords are driving conversions in paid search\n\n   but are completely missing from our organic positioning\n\nGive me a prioritized list of copy changes — specific headline or\n\nsubheadline rewrites that close the gap between how buyers describe\n\ntheir problem and how we describe our solution.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## **Going Deeper**\n\n**Extend to key landing pages:**\n\n```plain text\n\nRun the same language gap analysis for our [pricing / product / about]\n\npage. Compare the page copy against the search queries and paid keywords\n\nthat drive traffic specifically to that URL.\n\n```\n\n**Validate with competitor language:**\n\n```plain text\n\nResearch the homepage copy of our top 3 competitors. Which buyer-language\n\nkeywords are they using in their headlines that we're not? Are there terms\n\nappearing in both competitor copy and our search queries that we're ignoring?\n\n```\n\n**A/B test brief:**\n\n```plain text\n\nBased on the language gap analysis, write two alternative versions of our\n\nhomepage headline and subheadline. Version A uses our current language.\n\nVersion B incorporates the highest-intent buyer language identified.\n\nFormat as a brief for our design team.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## **Pro Tips**\n\n1. **Paid search terms are the most revealing data point.** These aren't casual browsers — they're people who typed something specific because they wanted to buy something specific. If those words aren't on your homepage, you have a friction problem that no amount of ad spend can fully compensate for.\n\n2. **Words on your homepage that never appear in search queries are internal language that leaked into customer-facing copy.** Your buyers don't use those words because they've never heard them. Flag every one for removal or translation.\n\n3. **For sharper long-tail insights,** pull the raw Search Terms Report separately from the Google Ads UI (Reports → Predefined Reports → Search Terms, filter conversions \\> 0, last 90 days) and layer it in as a follow-up prompt.\n\n---\n\n## **Common Mistakes**\n\n**Changing copy based on volume alone.** A keyword with 10 searches/month that converts at 15% is worth more than one with 10,000 searches that never converts.\n\n**Treating this as a one-time fix.** Search language shifts. Run this quarterly to keep homepage copy aligned with how buyers are talking right now.\n\n**Ignoring informational queries.** Some of your highest-volume GSC queries are informational, not commercial. Don't stuff homepage copy with research-stage terms that attract the wrong visitors.\n\n---\n\n## **What to Do With the Results**\n\n**This week:**\n\n1. Run the full language gap analysis\n\n2. Flag the top 5 copy changes with the strongest evidence\n\n**This quarter:**\n\n3. Implement the highest-priority changes\n\n4. Monitor GSC CTR and conversion rate on the homepage after changes go live\n\n5. Schedule the next review for 90 days out", "has_succeeded": true, "artifacts": []}

