data: {"type": "action", "id": "act18", "icon": "file-text", "title": "Writing Homepage Language Gap Analysis", "results": [], "started_at": "2026-08-01T09:03:32.664425Z", "ended_at": "2026-08-01T09:04:31.219328Z", "input": {"path": "outputs/Homepage Language Gap Analysis.md", "content": "# Homepage Language Gap Analysis — example.com\n**Run date:** 2026-06-18 | **Data window:** Mar 19 – Jun 18, 2026 (90 days)\n\n---\n\n## Data Sources\n\n| Source | Scope | Key signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Google Search Console | Top 50 queries by clicks | Organic discovery language |\n| Google Ads | Top 26 keywords with >0 conversions (of 289 total) | Buyer-intent, purchase-stage language |\n| Homepage copy | Live scrape of example.com, 2026-06-18 | Current language baseline |\n\n---\n\n## Finding 1: High-Converting Keywords Missing from Homepage Copy\n\nThese are the words buyers typed right before converting via paid search — the highest-quality signal available. None of these phrases appear in the homepage headline, subheadline, or section labels.\n\n| Keyword | Match Type | Conversions | Missing from homepage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| marketing intelligence software | BROAD | **128** | \"software\", \"intelligence platform/software\" framing |\n| ai marketing intelligence | PHRASE | **96** | Full phrase absent |\n| marketing intelligence platform | PHRASE | **76** | \"platform\" entirely absent from homepage |\n| b2b marketing automation platforms | BROAD | **34** | \"automation\", \"platform\", \"b2b\" all absent |\n| ai marketing software | PHRASE | **29** | \"software\" absent |\n| marketing operations platform | PHRASE | **15** | \"operations\", \"platform\" absent |\n| marketing data platform | BROAD | **14** | \"data platform\" absent |\n| unified marketing analytics | BROAD | **11** | \"analytics\", \"unified\" absent |\n| marketing analytics software | PHRASE | **10** | \"analytics\", \"software\" absent |\n| marketing workflow automation | BROAD | **4** | \"automation\" absent from headline context |\n\n**Total conversions from terms with no homepage representation: 417 out of 426**\n\nThe three dominant clusters — **\"marketing intelligence\"**, **\"platform/software\"**, and **\"automation\"** — are all invisible on the homepage. DOJO is spending money to attract buyers who use these words, then landing them on a page that doesn't speak their language.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 2: Homepage Language That Buyers Never Use\n\nThese are words and phrases that appear prominently on the homepage but have zero presence in any search query (paid or organic) in the last 90 days. They are internal concepts that have leaked into customer-facing copy.\n\n| Homepage phrase | Where it appears | Search query presence |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"compounding intelligence\" | Section header, repeated | Zero queries |\n| \"living graph\" | Feature section | Zero queries |\n| \"proactive agents\" | Feature section | Zero queries |\n| \"always on-capture\" | Feature section label | Zero queries |\n| \"compounding loop\" | Feature diagram | Zero queries |\n| \"intelligent marketing system\" | **Main headline** | Zero queries |\n| \"one source of truth\" | Section header | Zero queries |\n| \"system of record\" | (Positioning docs) | Zero queries |\n\nThe headline itself — \"The intelligent marketing system\" — matches zero search queries. Buyers search for \"marketing intelligence\" (a noun-forward category descriptor), not \"intelligent marketing\" (an adjective-forward internal positioning label). This is the single most consequential gap in the page.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 3: Buyer-Intent Terms Converting in Paid But Absent from Organic Positioning\n\nThe organic (GSC) non-branded queries show what buyers type when they're researching — not yet in purchase mode. The paid data shows what converts. The overlap is where the real prize is.\n\n**Organic non-branded queries with meaningful volume:**\n| Query | Clicks | Avg Position |\n|---|---|---|\n| marketing os | 35 | 6.0 |\n| marketing operating system | 25 | 5.6 |\n| ai marketing operating system | 9 | 5.4 |\n| what is a marketing operating system | 3 | 1.8 |\n| ai marketing os | 3 | 9.9 |\n| marketingos | 2 | 6.9 |\n\n**Critical observation:** DOJO ranks well for \"marketing OS/operating system\" organically but these terms barely appear on the homepage and aren't reflected in the conversion-driving paid keywords at all. That means: DOJO built an organic foothold in a category (\"marketing OS\") that doesn't convert, while the categories that do convert (\"marketing intelligence platform\", \"marketing analytics software\") have almost no organic presence.\n\nThis is a positioning fragmentation problem. The homepage needs to bridge these two vocabularies — or commit to one.\n\n---\n\n## Prioritized Copy Changes\n\n### Priority 1 — Main Headline (Highest impact)\n\n**Current:** \"The intelligent marketing system\"\n\n**The problem:** Zero buyers search \"intelligent marketing system.\" The converting category is \"marketing intelligence [platform/software].\" Word order is not trivial here — \"intelligent marketing\" reads as an adjective-modified noun; \"marketing intelligence\" reads as a recognized software category.\n\n**Recommended rewrite:**\n> **The marketing intelligence platform that compounds.**\n\nOr, if you want to keep the \"system\" framing alive:\n> **The AI marketing intelligence system.**\n\n**Why this works:** Preserves DOJO's differentiation claim (\"compounds\"/\"system\") while front-loading the category language buyers actually use (128 + 96 + 76 = 300 conversions on these three terms alone).\n\n---\n\n### Priority 2 — Subheadline\n\n**Current:** \"DOJO watches every channel, tracks your brand, runs your workflows, and acts before you have to. Getting smarter every time.\"\n\n**The problem:** No \"intelligence,\" no \"platform,\" no \"analytics,\" no \"automation.\" The subheadline uses poetic internal framing but delivers zero keyword reinforcement.\n\n**Recommended rewrite:**\n> **One platform for marketing intelligence, campaign analytics, and workflow automation — connected across every channel, and getting smarter with every outcome.**\n\n**Why this works:** Surfaces three high-converting keyword clusters (\"intelligence,\" \"analytics,\" \"automation\") in one sentence. \"Connected\" bridges the DOJO graph concept without using internal jargon. \"Every outcome\" preserves the compounding narrative.\n\n---\n\n### Priority 3 — Meta Title and Description\n\n**Current title:** \"DOJO AI - The Intelligent Marketing System\"\n**Current description:** \"DOJO AI watches every channel, tracks your brand, runs your workflows, and acts before you have to. Getting smarter every time.\"\n\n**The problem:** The title uses \"Intelligent Marketing System\" which no buyer searches for. The description carries no keyword value for the converting terms.\n\n**Recommended title:**\n> DOJO AI — Marketing Intelligence Platform\n\n**Recommended description:**\n> The AI marketing intelligence platform for teams who want unified analytics, automated workflows, and compounding results. Connect every channel. Act before you have to.\n\n---\n\n### Priority 4 — \"Proactive Dashboard\" Section Label\n\n**Current label:** \"PROACTIVE DASHBOARD\"\n\n**Recommended:** \"UNIFIED MARKETING ANALYTICS\"\n\n\"Unified marketing analytics\" drove 11 conversions on its own and maps directly to what the dashboard section actually shows (cross-channel metrics in one view). \"Proactive\" is an internal descriptor that adds friction.\n\n---\n\n### Priority 5 — \"Pre-Built Workflows\" Section Label\n\n**Current label:** \"PRE-BUILT WORKFLOWS\"\n\n**Recommended:** \"MARKETING WORKFLOW AUTOMATION\"\n\n\"Marketing workflow automation\" is a direct buyer search term. \"Pre-built\" is a feature descriptor, not a category signal. Keeping \"workflows\" while adding \"automation\" closes the gap with 38+ converting queries in that cluster.\n\n---\n\n### Priority 6 — Add B2B Qualifier\n\n**b2b marketing automation platforms** drove 34 conversions. The homepage has no \"B2B\" anywhere visible. If DOJO's primary buyer is a B2B marketer (which the ICP confirms), a single line in the hero — even in supporting copy or a badge — pays off disproportionately.\n\n**Suggested placement:** Below the hero CTA or in the pricing anchor section:\n> \"Built for B2B and B2C marketing teams at growing companies.\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary Matrix\n\n| Change | Conversion signal | Effort | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rewrite main headline | 300 conversions on matching terms | Low | 🔴 P0 |\n| Rewrite subheadline | 417 conversions across all gaps | Low | 🔴 P0 |\n| Update meta title/description | 300+ conversions, also affects SEO CTR | Low | 🔴 P0 |\n| Rename dashboard section | 11 conversions; also improves SEO | Low | 🟠 P1 |\n| Rename workflows section | 38 conversions across automation cluster | Low | 🟠 P1 |\n| Add B2B qualifier | 34 conversions | Low | 🟠 P1 |\n| Retire \"living graph\", \"compounding loop\" from headers | Internal language — no buyer signal | Medium | 🟡 P2 |\n| Add \"analytics\" language to revenue section | 21 conversions on analytics terms | Medium | 🟡 P2 |\n\n---\n\n## What to Watch After Changes Go Live\n\n1. **GSC CTR on homepage** — headline/meta changes should improve click-through from branded impressions and any non-branded impressions where DOJO appears.\n2. **Paid CPA on \"marketing intelligence\" cluster** — better landing page language match should improve Quality Score and reduce CPA.\n3. **Organic ranking for \"marketing intelligence platform\"** — currently not in the top 50 GSC queries; this is the category DOJO should own.\n4. **Run this analysis again in 90 days** to measure language shift.\n"}, "kind": "library"}

